## Examples

### Good: Complete Implementation
```go
func ProcessData(data []byte) ([]byte, error) {
    if len(data) == 0 {
        return nil, fmt.Errorf("empty data provided")
    }
    
    processed := make([]byte, len(data))
    for i, b := range data {
        processed[i] = b ^ 0xFF // Simple transformation
    }
    
    return processed, nil
}
```

### Bad: Placeholder Implementation
```go
func ProcessData(data []byte) ([]byte, error) {
    // TODO: Implement data processing
    return nil, fmt.Errorf("not implemented")
}
```

### Good: Complete Error Handling
```go
func ValidateConfig(config *Config) error {
    if config == nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("config cannot be nil")
    }
    
    if config.Name == "" {
        return fmt.Errorf("config name is required")
    }
    
    if config.Port <= 0 || config.Port > 65535 {
        return fmt.Errorf("invalid port number: %d", config.Port)
    }
    
    return nil
}
```

### Bad: Stub with Panic
```go
func ValidateConfig(config *Config) error {
    panic("not implemented")
}
```

This rule ensures that the spooky codebase maintains high quality, functional code and avoids accumulating technical debt from incomplete implementations.

